{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 0d7d8fa028c2e266ee4489e3f49d5a69ed6ef1f4. This head touches no card path, so the citation rule of 0998f9ec does not apply: I checked the diff from main against the workflow's path list, and no path matches. No run used two machines or a desktop AI app, as your note states.\n\n**`inviteDevice`: right.** It checks the signer's role when it can read one, checks that the member is active, and proves that the link encodes **before** it signs, because the secret is not kept. A failure after the act says that the invitation took effect, and tells the person to make another link. The scope still judges the act, so the local refusal is a courtesy and no authority.\n\n**`deviceBindings`: right.** It follows the invitation's fact, requires `invite-key` of kind `device` with fields that match this link's secret, and requires that the member is active, the handle equals the link's, and the key is active, of kind `device`, this key's own ID, and that member's. So a relabelled link gives no usable connection, and your `handle-binding` control holds it. `inboxOfMember` reads only that member's own entries, and `inboxOfOpening` checks the birth and the owner, so a link cannot point a new device at another inbox.\n\n**Custody.** The new key is made on the second home and saved before the send. The first home does not hold it. On the pins that use an invitation key, `enrol` carries a `proof` bound to the joining key, so the secret never enters the entry, as your test holds at `:96` and `:158`.\n\nOne qualification: the list you accept, `isAgentKeyMembership`, also holds @2, @4 and @5. On those pins `invitationRedemptionFields` gives `{ invitation, secret }`, so the signed request and the entry do hold the secret, as an agent key does there already. Your claim \"no membership entry holds the invitation's credential\" ran on the newer pins, and it does not reach those. dap-lending's rooms sit on an older register, so this is worth a sentence at the next edit. It does not hold this head, because that secret is single-use and enrolling consumes it.\n\n**The pending enrolment.** Your request says that it clears \"for every kind\". The code clears it in the `device-key` branch only, and your note says correctly that the agent path keeps it. The note is right and the request's words are not, so nothing needs changing in the head.\n\n**The shared line.** `isAgentKeyMembership` now guards all three kinds. An agent link's codec already held that list, and `CHECKER_KEY_MEMBERSHIP` is @8. The gate, which runs the checker's tests, passes. I did not trace the checker path further.\n\n**The scene and its labels are honest.** Two homes on one Mac stand for two machines, the deployed scene ran at 11:27 EST on the version then live, and its needs-you card was empty, so the row and the approval under the second key come from the local scene. No run here redeems a command-line link on the Page in a browser. Your test runs the page's data functions with no browser, and 756bb8b9 owes that run.\n\n**The planner's questions: my view, under 32f19d16.** None of the three is a false sentence about a send, so all can wait. A used directory after a refused link already gets words that say what to do. The MCP `join` tool refusing a device link is a real limit only if a person with only `dap mcp` needs a second device, and J5's scene does not. The page's hint is the one I would take first, at that page's next edit, since it omits a route that now exists.\n\n**Evidence.** `SHA256SUMS` is 7e74eb37…, every digest verifies, and no file is writable. The gate's `head.txt` is 5718e09c, exit 0, with 3535 and 1022 tests. All 6 controls report `DISTINGUISHES`. The merge of main after the gate touches no file of this head, and the note is what changes on this side. I ran `device-key.scope.test.ts`, `agent-key.scope.test.ts` and `join.scope.test.ts` (14 passed), `assets.test.ts` (1 passed), `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (1002, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (5, 0 flagged). The worktree is clean. I did not rerun the controls, the scene or the deployed run.\n\n**Architecture.** Extending `enrolKey` keeps one place for key, envelope and settlement custody. No route, definition or Worker behaviour changes.\n\n**Security.** A link is a bearer credential for one member's new key, printed once and good for 24 hours by default. The docs say to give it to that member only. A person who holds the link can enrol a key for that member, as on the page today, so this head adds a route to an existing power and no new power. Only a role with `membership.invite` can make one.\n\n**Simplification.** Nothing to remove. `deviceBindings` is the one added check, and only the device path needs it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bf2b13ed31f8ec534b9a0fe7b36a0607d10bdea4","binding":"assigned","head":"0d7d8fa028c2e266ee4489e3f49d5a69ed6ef1f4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f67866eb577c413468fbb13c88778114bb64b89c\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:de98530cd4f7ed57fccaa20d2a79983efee02720\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:12f75101d0fc9494769afa3c7ca88dec4742146f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}